Intellectus data protection by design/default checklist

Reviewer: Elza Paegle
Rule: Pass requires evidence. Unknown is a launch gap, not an acceptable assumption.

Control

Status

Current finding

Required remediation

Bounded purpose and research scope

Partial

Organisational diagnostic preparation is defined, but person-level necessity is not documented.

Approve each use case; prefer organisation-level evidence; prohibit people-ranking and unrelated profiling.

Data minimisation and sensitive-data exclusion

Fail

Public sources may contain unnecessary Article 9 or Article 10 data.

Limit sources/windows; minimise names and quotations; filter or quarantine sensitive and offence data.

Controller, processor and subprocessor map

Fail

Client, Intellectus operator, n8n and API roles are not fully confirmed.

Document each entity, purpose, instruction, location and contract; obtain legal review where roles overlap.

Lawful basis and Article 14 plan

Fail

No approved LIA or indirect-collection transparency plan is evidenced.

Complete the Article 6(1)(f) three-part test and record notice delivery or a case-specific Article 14(5)(b) assessment.

Source and inference integrity

Partial

Source/evidence links and epistemic labels are strong, but production QA is incomplete.

Preserve labels; verify identity and sources; block unsupported findings from a Ready brief.

Meaningful consultant review

Partial

The consultant controls questions, next steps, notes and Draft/Ready status.

Require a review checklist, reviewer identity and recorded outcome; train reviewers on privacy and sensitive data.

Rights-ready traceability

Fail

No process shows how to find and act on a person's data across runs and vendors.

Link person/source/evidence/finding/run; test access, correction, objection, restriction and deletion.

Retention and deletion

Unknown

Browser state resets, but n8n executions, logs, APIs and backups are undefined.

Approve purpose-based periods; configure automatic deletion and run cancellation; obtain vendor evidence.

No training or secondary use

Unknown

Provider settings and contracts are not evidenced.

Execute Article 28 terms; disable training; prohibit cross-client reuse and unauthorised human review.

Production security

Fail

Direct browser-to-webhook deployment cannot protect a secret; authentication is not implemented.

Use an authenticated backend/gateway, strict CORS, rate/body limits, least privilege, encryption and redacted logs.

Transfers and subprocessors

Unknown

Processing and support locations are not fully mapped.

Approve subprocessors and document any adequacy decision, SCCs, transfer assessment and supplementary measures.

DPIA and accountability

Fail

Architecture evidence exists, but no approved DPIA screening, ROPA or complete privacy file is supplied.

Complete the threshold assessment, LIA, ROPA, notices, DPAs, security review, rights test and sign-offs.

Launch gate

Result: fail pending remediation. Production may proceed only when every Fail is closed and each Unknown is resolved with evidence. Minimum approval requires: confirmed roles and vendors; an approved LIA and Article 14 approach; purpose and sensitive-data controls; Article 28 and transfer terms; secured webhook architecture; retention/deletion settings; tested individual-rights workflows; formal consultant review; and an approved DPIA threshold decision. If likely high risk or high residual risk remains, complete the full DPIA and apply Article 36 where required.

This is an educational first-pass review, not legal advice or certification.
